{{Quote|Master Jabba&mdash;a boon! Please&hellip;spare the rancor. He is a gentle beast and has done no wrong.''»<br />«''Very well, Malakiki. I, too, am fond of the creature. Your pet may live.|translated=1|Malakili and Jabba the Hutt|<ref name="Star Wars 39">[[Star Wars (2020) 39|''Star Wars'' (2020) 39]]</ref>}}
'''Pateesa'''<ref name="Aftermath: Life Debt">''[[Aftermath: Life Debt]]''</ref> ([[Huttese]] for "friend") was a [[Gender|male]] [[rancor]]<ref name="AEYNTK" /> owned by the [[Hutt]] [[crime lord]] [[Jabba Desilijic Tiure]] after his [[majordomo]] [[Bib Fortuna]] gave it to him as a [[birthday]] gift.<ref name="CEUE">''[[Star Wars Character Encyclopedia: Updated and Expanded]]''</ref> He was kept as Jabba's pet in a shadowy chamber beneath the Hutt's throne room in [[Boba Fett's Palace|his palace]] on the [[desert]] [[planet]] [[Tatooine]],<ref name="ROTJ" /> where the rancor was cared for by the [[human]] keeper [[Malakili]].<ref name="USW" /> Jabba used Pateesa to feast on the [[gangster]]'s enemies, most notably the [[Jedi Knight]] [[Luke Skywalker]] during a [[Rescue of Han Solo|mission]] to rescue his friend and fellow [[Alliance to Restore the Republic|Rebel]] [[Han Solo]]. Through a trap door, the Hutt laughed and dispensed the young Jedi into the underground dungeon and unleashed the massive rancor. But to the shock of Jabba and his thugs, Luke was able to kill the beast, leading the outraged gangster to order the public execution of the Jedi and his friends.<ref name="ROTJ" />

==Biography==
===Jabba's pet===
{{Quote|Oh no! The rancor!|[[C-3PO]]|<ref name="ROTJ" />}}
[[File:FineArtPrints-The-Rancor.png|thumb|left|250px|Pateesa in his cavern]]
Pateesa<ref name="Aftermath: Life Debt" /> was given as a birthday gift<ref name="USW" /> to [[Jabba Desilijic Tiure]] by a high-ranking henchman,<ref name="Complete Locations">[[Star Wars: Complete Locations (2016)|''Star Wars: Complete Locations'']]</ref> [[Bib Fortuna]],<ref name="USW" /> whose one-time competitor for the position of [[majordomo]], [[Bidlo Kwerve]], became an early victim of the [[rancor]].<ref name="Complete Locations" /> Jabba kept Pateesa in a shadowy chamber beneath his Tatooine throne room, having often fed his enemies or those who upset him to the rancor and enjoyed watching those victims being devoured.<ref name="VE">''[[Star Wars: The Visual Encyclopedia]]''</ref> In [[3 ABY]],<ref name="OutlawsDate">{{OutlawsDate}}</ref> thief [[Kay Vess]] was caught infiltrating Jabba’s Palace. She, [[Vail Tormin]] and [[Hoss]] were dropped in the Rancor pit. After Hoss died, Tormin and Vess escaped, but Pateesa chased after them. The rancor eventually cornered them in the palace’s hanger, but with the help of rail-powered explosives, they were able to anger Pateesa enough and he incapacitated himself when he smashed his head into the hangar door three times. Sometime before this encounter Malakili sent a message to Bib Fortuna that Pateesa shouldn't be fed more [[bounty hunter]]s due to the [[detonator]]s on their persons. Suggesting instead that Pateesa needed at least ten more days of good meat and grain.<ref name="Outlaws" /> When the [[Twi'lek]] dancer [[Oola]] displeased the Hutt shortly before the [[Battle of Endor]] in [[4 ABY]], after she had enough of following his orders and entertaining him, Jabba dropped her into the pit below and watched her [[death]] of being eaten by Pateesa.<ref name="ROTJ" />

[[File:LukeDefendsHimselfAgainstPateesa-SWROTJVA.png|thumb|right|250px|Luke armed with a bone, defends himself against Pateesa]]

Shortly after Oola's death, [[Jedi Knight]] [[Luke Skywalker]] arrived at the palace with the [[Rescue of Han Solo|intent]] of rescuing his friend [[Han Solo]], whom Jabba held captive. However, Jabba rejected the Skywalker's demands, dispatched him into the pit along with the unlucky [[Gamorrean]] guard [[Jubnuk]], and unleashed Pateesa. The young Jedi [[Commander]] backed away from the terrifying rancor while the Gamorrean tried futilely to scramble out of the pit. Pateesa quickly grabbed the guard and ate him, but Skywalker proved to be more of a challenge: after being seized in one of the beast's huge hands, the Jedi placed a large femur between his jaws before he could be devoured, forcing the rancor to let him go. Luke's effort was only a temporary delay as the beast quickly snapped the bone in two. Luke retreated under a rock outcropping and the rancor reached for him again. The Jedi smashed his finger with a rock, prompting the animal to bellow in pain. Luke then took the opportunity to run around and behind the beast, into the holding pen where it lived while being cared for. Luke found himself unable to escape through the back gate of the chamber. Out of options due to the rancor's handlers refusing to open the gate, Luke waited until the rancor followed him into its holding pen and smashed the control panel with the skull<ref name="ROTJ" /> belonging to Bidlo Kwerve,<ref name="Complete Locations" /> dropping the heavy door, piercing and crushing the beast's skull. As Pateesa died, he moaned weakly, with Malakili rushing to his aid, before breaking down in tears.<ref name="ROTJ" />

===Legacy===
{{Quote|Bring me Solo and the Wookiee. They will all suffer for this outrage.|Jabba Desilijic Tiure, responding to Pateesa's unexpected death|translated=1|audio=JabbaTheyWillAllSuffer.flac|<ref name="ROTJ" />}}
To the shock of Jabba and his thugs, a mere [[human]] was able to kill the beast. Pateesa's death greatly upset Jabba, who immediately demanded Skywalker and his companions be executed at the [[Great Pit of Carkoon]]. However, Jabba's decision to underestimate Skywalker again ultimately sealed his fate, as he perished above the pit whereas the [[Jedi]] and his allies escaped.<ref name="ROTJ" />

[[File:RIP_Pateesa.jpg|thumb|right|250px|Pateesa's corpse]]
Malakili was devastated by the rancor's death, believing that he had failed him and no longer had purpose in life. The trainer was also bitter towards Skywalker for killing his pet and companion. Malakili found a new purpose in life when he encountered the vigilante lawman and [[Mayor]] of [[Freetown]] [[Cobb Vanth]], who enlisted his services in looking after the Huttlet [[Borgo]] and taming two [[ronto]]s.<ref name="Aftermath: Life Debt" />

By [[9 ABY]],<ref name="BoBFDate">{{BoBFDate}}</ref> Pateesa's corpse was no longer in the rancor pit.<ref name="The Tribes of Tatooine">{{BOBF|Chapter 2: The Tribes of Tatooine}}</ref> A new [[Boba's rancor|rancor]] was gifted to [[Boba Fett]] by Jabba's cousins, [[the Twins]], as an apology for trying to [[Assassination attempt on Boba Fett|kill]] him. The new rancor resided in Pateesa's old rancor pit.<ref name="Streets of Mos Espa">{{BOBF|Chapter 3: The Streets of Mos Espa}}</ref>

==Behind the scenes==
{{Quote|I like the idea that everyone loves someone. And even the worst, most horrible monster you can imagine was loved by his keeper. And the rancor probably loved his keeper.|[[George Lucas]] on Pateesa's relationship with Malakili|<ref name="Making of ROTJ">''[[The Making of Return of the Jedi]]''</ref>}}
[[File:Rancor_suit_btm.jpg|thumb|left|250px|Pateesa was originally to be portrayed by a man in a suit.]]
Pateesa, then identified simply as Jabba's rancor, first appeared in the [[1983]] film [[Star Wars: Episode VI Return of the Jedi|''Star Wars'': Episode VI ''Return of the Jedi'']]<ref name="ROTJ" /> and was later identified in [[Chuck Wendig]]'s [[2016]] novel ''[[Aftermath: Life Debt]]''.<ref name="Aftermath: Life Debt" /> The ''[[Star Wars: The Bad Batch]]'' episode "[[Rampage (episode)|Rampage]]" later introduced a rancor in Jabba's care named [[Muchi]].<ref name="Rampage">{{TBB|Rampage (episode)}}</ref> ''[[LEGO Star Wars: The Skywalker Saga]]'' claimed Muchi and Pateesa were the same rancor by naming the rancor carried by Malakili as "Pateesa Muchi."<ref name="LEGO">''[[LEGO Star Wars: The Skywalker Saga]]''</ref> However, Pateesa is identified as a male by ''[[Star Wars: Absolutely Everything You Need to Know]]'',<ref name="AEYNTK" /> while Muchi was established to be a female in "Rampage."<ref name="Rampage" />

[[File:Rancorvsluke.jpg|thumb|right|250px|Art by [[Ralph McQuarrie]].]]
Originally, Pateesa was intended to be a man in a suit, but the results were not satisfyingly realistic, so a rod puppet was designed and constructed for filming.<ref name="RotJ:BtS">{{SW|url=return-of-the-jedi-behind-the-scenes|text=''Star Wars: Return of the Jedi'': Behind the Scenes Gallery}} {{C|Slide 7}}</ref> For the base audio of the rancor's roars and howls, [[Ben Burtt]] recorded his neighbor's cute but aggressive [[Wikipedia:Dachshund|dachshund]] named Max, then pitched the audio down.<ref name="ROTJ Commentary">Audio commentary on [[Star Wars: Episode VI Return of the Jedi|''Star Wars'': Episode VI ''Return of the Jedi'']] [[Star Wars Trilogy (DVD)|2004 DVD]]</ref>

An image often mistaken to be Pateesa battling a [[krayt dragon]] is actually a joke from the crew of [[Industrial Light & Magic]]. They placed a model of Vermithrax Pejorative, the dragon from the [[1981]] film [[Wikipedia:Dragonslayer|''Dragonslayer'']], in the arms of the rancor model and took a picture. The picture was included in the [[1997]] behind-the-scenes book [[Star Wars Chronicles|''Star Wars: Chronicles'']].<ref name="SW:C">''[[Star Wars Chronicles]]''</ref>

